# ---------------------------------------------------------------- guides (resources)
GUIDES = [
  dict(slug="google-business-profile-checklist", seo="Google Business Profile checklist 2026", cat="Local search", title="The complete Google Business Profile checklist for 2026", lede="Most profiles are half-finished. Here's every field that matters, in order, and what to put in it.", read="7 min",
       sections=[
         ("Why the profile matters more than your website", "<p>For a local business, the Google Business Profile is usually the first thing a customer sees — in Maps, in the local pack, and in the knowledge panel beside search results. It drives calls, direction requests and website taps before anyone reaches your site. It's also the anchor that AI assistants use to understand what your business is. A complete, active profile outperforms a merely claimed one on every one of those measures.</p>"),
         ("1. Claim, verify and lock it down", "<ul><li>Claim the profile at business.google.com and complete verification (video verification is now common; have your signage, equipment and a business document ready).</li><li>Add a second owner so access never depends on one person's Gmail.</li><li>Turn on two-step verification for every owner.</li><li>Remove duplicate profiles for the same location — they split your reviews and confuse Google.</li></ul>"),
         ("2. Name, categories and attributes", "<ul><li><b>Name:</b> your real business name, exactly as it appears on your signage. No keywords stuffed in — Google suspends profiles for it.</li><li><b>Primary category:</b> the single most specific category for what you do. This is the strongest ranking signal on the profile.</li><li><b>Secondary categories:</b> add every category that genuinely applies (a hotel with a restaurant: Hotel + Restaurant + Bar).</li><li><b>Attributes:</b> fill in everything — accessibility, amenities, payment types, service options. They appear in filters and in AI answers.</li></ul>"),
         ("3. Hours, phone, website and links", "<ul><li>Set regular hours and <b>special hours</b> for every holiday for the year ahead. “Hours might differ” is a lost customer.</li><li>Use a local phone number that someone answers.</li><li>Link the website homepage (or a location page for multi-location businesses), and add booking, ordering, menu and appointment links where offered.</li></ul>"),
         ("4. Services, products and menu", "<p>Add every service you offer with a short description and price range where you can. Restaurants: add the menu through the menu editor, not just a photo. Retail: add products. These fields feed the “services” and “products” tabs and give Google text to match against searches.</p>"),
         ("5. Photos and video", "<ul><li>Logo and cover photo, then at least 20 photos: exterior (so people recognize it from the street), interior, team, work, products.</li><li>Add new photos monthly. Profiles with recent photos get more direction requests.</li><li>Short videos (under 30 seconds) of the space or the work perform well.</li></ul>"),
         ("6. Posts, Q&A and messaging", "<ul><li>Post weekly: an update, an offer or an event. Posts expire from view, so consistency matters more than polish.</li><li>Seed the Q&A with the five questions customers ask most, and answer them yourself.</li><li>Turn on messaging only if someone will answer within a few hours; otherwise leave it off.</li></ul>"),
         ("7. Reviews", "<p>Ask every customer, automatically, after every visit — by text or email with the direct review link. Reply to every review within a business day. Rating, recency, volume and response rate all influence ranking, and all four influence the customer reading them.</p>"),
         ("8. Check it monthly", "<p>Google changes categories, adds attributes and sometimes accepts public “edits” to your profile. Once a month: check the facts, read the insights (searches, calls, directions), add photos, and compare your rating and review count with the three competitors above and below you in the map.</p>")],
       cta=("Want it done for you?", "Local listings management is included in every Landmark Digital plan, or on its own from $499/mo with local SEO.")),
  dict(slug="get-more-google-reviews", seo="How to get more Google reviews", cat="Reputation", title="How to get more Google reviews (without breaking the rules)", lede="A system that asks every customer, every time — and why that's better than any incentive.", read="6 min",
       sections=[
         ("The math of reviews", "<p>A business with a 4.7 rating and 300 recent reviews beats a 4.9 with 40 old ones, in Google's ranking and in the customer's mind. Recency and volume signal that you're busy and consistent. The only reliable way to get both is to ask everyone, every time, automatically.</p>"),
         ("What's allowed and what isn't", "<ul><li><b>Allowed:</b> asking customers to leave a review, sending a direct link, putting a QR code on the counter, asking in person.</li><li><b>Not allowed:</b> paying or discounting for reviews, asking only happy customers (“review gating”), writing reviews yourself or through friends, buying reviews. Google removes these and can suspend the profile; the FTC fines for it.</li></ul>"),
         ("Build the ask into the visit", "<ol><li>Get the direct review link from your Google Business Profile (“Ask for reviews”).</li><li>Send it automatically after each visit — a text 2–4 hours later works best for restaurants and services; a next-day email for hotels and practices.</li><li>Keep the message short and personal: “Thanks for coming in today, Maria. If you have a minute, a Google review helps us more than you'd think: [link]”.</li><li>Add a QR code card at the counter, on the table or on the invoice for people who'd rather do it now.</li><li>Train staff to mention it once, at the natural moment (checkout, check-out, job complete).</li></ol>"),
         ("Reply to every one", "<p>Replies are read by the next customer more than by the reviewer. Thank the good ones specifically (“glad the patio worked for the birthday”). For negative ones: acknowledge, don't argue, take it offline (“please call me directly at…”), and fix the thing if it's real. A calm reply to a bad review is the most persuasive content on your profile.</p>"),
         ("When a review breaks the rules", "<p>Reviews that are spam, off-topic, from a competitor or a non-customer, or that contain hate or personal information can be flagged for removal in the profile. It works more often than people think, but it takes days to weeks — reply in the meantime.</p>"),
         ("Beyond Google", "<p>Google first, then the platform your industry lives on: TripAdvisor for hotels, Yelp for restaurants, Healthgrades for practices, Facebook for local services. Alternate your requests so you build all of them.</p>")],
       cta=("Reputation management from $199/mo", "Automated requests, every review answered within a business day, and a monthly report with competitor comparison.")),
  dict(slug="website-speed-local-business", seo="Why website speed decides who calls", cat="Websites", title="Why your website's speed decides whether people call", lede="Half of mobile visitors leave if a page takes more than three seconds. Here's what slows local business sites down and how to fix it.", read="6 min",
       sections=[
         ("Speed is a trust signal", "<p>A visitor on a phone, standing in a parking lot, gives your site about three seconds. If the menu or the phone number isn't on screen by then, they go back to the search results and tap the next business. Google measures this (Core Web Vitals) and uses it in ranking; customers measure it by leaving.</p>"),
         ("Test it properly", "<p>Run your homepage and your most important page through PageSpeed Insights on <b>mobile</b>. Look at Largest Contentful Paint (should be under 2.5 seconds) and Cumulative Layout Shift (under 0.1). Test on a real phone on cellular, not just your office Wi-Fi.</p>"),
         ("The usual culprits", "<ul><li><b>Huge images.</b> A 4 MB photo straight from a phone is the #1 cause. Resize to the display size and compress (WebP).</li><li><b>Page builders and too many plugins.</b> Each adds scripts and styles to every page.</li><li><b>Sliders and video backgrounds</b> above the fold.</li><li><b>Cheap shared hosting</b> with slow server response.</li><li><b>Too many fonts</b> and third-party scripts (chat widgets, trackers, embeds).</li><li><b>No caching or CDN.</b></li></ul>"),
         ("Fixes in order of impact", "<ol><li>Compress and resize every image; lazy-load the ones below the fold.</li><li>Turn on caching (a good host does this) and a CDN.</li><li>Remove plugins you don't use; replace heavy ones.</li><li>Replace the hero slider with one strong image and a headline.</li><li>Move to managed WordPress hosting if server response is over 600 ms.</li><li>Load third-party scripts after the page is interactive, or drop them.</li></ol>"),
         ("What good looks like", "<p>Under two seconds to interactive on 4G, the phone number and primary action visible without scrolling, and a layout that doesn't jump as it loads. That's the standard every site we launch is tested against.</p>")],
       cta=("Not sure where you stand?", "The free digital audit includes a mobile speed test and the three fixes that would matter most.")),
  dict(slug="show-up-in-ai-search", seo="Get recommended by ChatGPT & Gemini", cat="AI search", title="How to get your business recommended by ChatGPT, Gemini and Perplexity", lede="AI assistants answer 'where should I go?' from sources they trust. Here's how to become one.", read="8 min",
       sections=[
         ("What's changed", "<p>A growing share of local questions — “best dentist near me that takes new patients”, “hotel near Universal Studios with free parking” — are now asked to AI assistants and to Google's AI Overviews. These answer with a short list of businesses and a reason for each. The businesses that appear share three things: consistent facts across the web, structured data on their site, and content that answers questions plainly.</p>"),
         ("1. Get the facts consistent everywhere", "<p>AI engines cross-check. If your hours differ between Google, Yelp and your site, or your name is spelled three ways, you're a risk to cite. Make name, address, phone, hours, categories and services identical on your site, Google Business Profile, Apple, Bing, Yelp, Facebook and industry directories.</p>"),
         ("2. Add structured data (schema)", "<p>Schema markup is a machine-readable summary of each page: this is a LocalBusiness, here are its services, prices, reviews, hours and FAQs. It's what lets an engine state “open until 9 pm, 4.8 stars, offers patio seating” with confidence. Mark up your organization, each service or menu, reviews and every FAQ.</p>"),
         ("3. Write answer-first content", "<p>Engines quote pages that answer a question in the first sentence. Rewrite key pages so the summary comes first: “Yes, we offer free parking for guests. The lot is behind the building…”. Build an FAQ page from the questions customers ask by phone, and keep it current.</p>"),
         ("4. Earn third-party mentions", "<p>Engines trust what others say about you more than what you say. Local press, chamber listings, partner websites, “best of” lists and guides all count. Pitch the local ones; they're easier than you think.</p>"),
         ("5. Measure it", "<p>Make a list of 10–20 questions your customers would ask an assistant. Once a month, ask each engine and record whether you're mentioned, where, and who else is. Watch for AI referrers (chatgpt.com, perplexity.ai) in your analytics. That's your GEO scorecard.</p>"),
         ("The good news", "<p>Everything above also improves traditional SEO and your Google Business Profile. There's no trade-off — it's the same work, done more carefully.</p>")],
       cta=("AI search visibility is included in Grow", "Or on its own for $299/mo: audit, schema, answer-first content and a monthly visibility report.")),
  dict(slug="restaurant-social-media-calendar", seo="Restaurant social media calendar", cat="Social media", title="A month of restaurant social media, planned in an hour", lede="A repeatable content calendar that fills a feed without a full-time marketer.", read="6 min",
       sections=[
         ("The problem with 'post when you can'", "<p>Feeds go quiet because nobody decided what Tuesday's post was. A calendar removes the decision: every week has the same shape, and you only fill in the specifics. One hour at the start of the month and a few phone clips during service are enough.</p>"),
         ("The weekly shape", "<ul><li><b>Monday — the dish.</b> One plate, shot in daylight by the window. Caption: what's in it, who makes it.</li><li><b>Wednesday — behind the scenes.</b> A 10-second clip from the kitchen or bar. Reels get reach; polish doesn't matter, motion does.</li><li><b>Friday — the reason to come this weekend.</b> Special, event, live music, patio weather.</li><li><b>Sunday — the people.</b> A regular, a staff member, a review screenshot (with permission).</li></ul>"),
         ("Shoot in batches", "<p>Pick one hour before service, once a week. Shoot four plates, two clips of the kitchen, one of the dining room filling up, one of a staff member doing something. That's two weeks of content. Vertical, natural light, no zoom.</p>"),
         ("Captions and hashtags", "<p>First line does the work — it's what shows before “more”. Say the specific thing: “The short rib is back on the menu through Sunday.” Five to eight hashtags: your city, your neighborhood, your cuisine, and two about the dish. Skip #foodporn-style tags with millions of posts.</p>"),
         ("Reply to everything", "<p>Comments and DMs are reservations waiting to happen. Reply the same day, in a human voice. Pin the best comment. Repost customers' stories that tag you.</p>"),
         ("Post the same things to Google", "<p>Your Friday “reason to come” post belongs on your Google Business Profile too, where people searching “dinner near me” will actually see it. One extra tap.</p>")],
       cta=("Would rather hand it off?", "Social media management from $399/mo: a calendar you approve, posts designed in your brand, and replies handled.")),
  dict(slug="hotel-direct-bookings-playbook", seo="Hotel direct-bookings playbook", cat="Hospitality", title="The direct-bookings playbook for independent hotels", lede="Seven moves that shift bookings from the OTAs to your own website — the approach behind 1,000+ direct bookings at Hotel Lexen.", read="9 min",
       sections=[
         ("Why direct matters", "<p>Every OTA booking costs 15–25% in commission and gives the guest relationship to someone else. You can't out-spend Expedia on search, but you don't have to: most guests check the hotel's own site before booking anyway. Your job is to make sure that visit ends in a booking.</p>"),
         ("1. Make the site booking-first", "<p>The booking widget — dates, guests, “check availability” — belongs at the top of every page, not behind a menu. One tap on a phone. Test it on your own phone, on cellular, monthly.</p>"),
         ("2. Promise the best rate, and mean it", "<p>“Best rate guaranteed when you book direct” plus a real perk (free parking, late check-out, a drink) beats a vague “book direct” badge. Make rate parity work for you by adding value, not just matching price.</p>"),
         ("3. Own the neighborhood", "<p>Guests search for what's near the hotel more than for the hotel. A neighborhood guide — studios, theaters, restaurants, transit, with walking times — earns search traffic you'd otherwise never see, and sells the stay better than room photos.</p>"),
         ("4. Complete the Google profile", "<p>Google's hotel panel shows rates from every channel. Make sure your direct rate is in it (through your booking engine's Google Hotel Ads connection), the photos are yours and recent, and the amenities and policies are filled in. Answer every review.</p>"),
         ("5. Capture and remarket", "<p>Most visitors don't book the first time. A simple “save this stay” email, retargeting ads for people who looked at rooms, and a pre-arrival email series for guests who did book all lift direct revenue.</p>"),
         ("6. Make the OTAs work for you", "<p>Use them for discovery, then win the guest: a welcome note with your site, a direct-booking perk for next time, a post-stay review request that links to Google rather than the OTA.</p>"),
         ("7. Measure direct share, not just occupancy", "<p>Track the share of bookings that come direct, month by month. That number — not website traffic — tells you whether the work is paying.</p>")],
       cta=("See the Hotel Lexen story", "A booking-first website and a complete Google presence: 1,000+ direct bookings in 12 months.")),
]
GUIDE_BY = {g["slug"]: g for g in GUIDES}

